{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce a bipartisan agreement whose provisions are included in the Collins-Lieberman bill, but the text says that bill is 'introduced today.' Under the negative clarification, introduction of a bill that has not advanced beyond introduction is not an achievement, and the boundary rule states a merely introduced bill is not an accomplishment. The provisions have not been enacted, passed by a chamber, or adopted in an advancing package, so no concrete outcome can be claimed. This is a close call because the release frames the negotiated compromise as an accomplishment, but under a strict reading of the boundary rule the package has not yet advanced beyond introduction."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The senator states in the first person: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is an explicit claim of responsibility for secured federal funding in a passed appropriations bill, squarely within clarification (1)/(4) (funding secured; touting past achievements with delivered results), even though much of the release praises the U.S. Marshals."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as a report on a visit to Blue Springs (an event), the release contains an explicit credit-claim: 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This ties Bond (co-author) to enacted legislation delivering concrete funds to Missouri, matching clarification (2) (authored legislation that was enacted) and (4) (touting past achievements with delivered results). The rest of the release is position-taking on Iraq, intelligence, and taxes."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified as a member of the Appropriations Committee, 'announced that the Department of Homeland Security will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department' through the Assistance to Firefighters Grant Program. Under clarification (1), announcing a federal grant benefiting the senator's state counts as credit-claiming whether the senator says 'I secured' or simply 'announced' the award — the announcement frame itself implies responsibility."
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "DeWine announces a $105,550 grant to the Parma Fire Department and explicitly links it to his own legislative work: he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' 'helped secure approximately $650 million' for it, and 'passed an updated version.' This combines the announcement frame (clarification 1) with claims of authored and enacted legislation and secured appropriations (clarifications 2 and 4) — a canonical credit-claiming release delivering federal funds to an Ohio community."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a floor statement criticizing Democrats' filibusters of judicial nominees and defending the constitutional option. It is pure position-taking and criticism of other actors (negative clarification 1), with commending of Leader Frist. No funding, legislation enacted, or other concrete outcome is claimed as Dole's achievement."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison signs on as an original cosponsor of the Veterans Housing Benefits Enhancement Act, which was 'introduced today.' Negative clarification (3) states that introduction or cosponsorship of bills that have not advanced beyond introduction is not credit-claiming. Her quoted statement ('will work with my colleagues to pass this measure') expresses support for future action, not an achieved outcome."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez states he cannot support the Pombo OCS bill in its current form, would join a filibuster, and applauds Congressman Putnam's efforts. This is position-taking on pending legislation and praise of another actor (negative clarifications 1 and 5/6). No government action or outcome is claimed as his achievement."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces that $8.1 million 'has been included' in the FY2006 MilCon-VA appropriations bill for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard, noting 'this funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' and that the bill 'passed the Senate today.' She also touts $23.8 billion in veterans funding she supported and letters requesting it. This fits clarification (1) (announced funding benefiting her state, credit shareable with a colleague) and the boundary rule treating provisions in a passed bill as claimed accomplishments."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The primary purpose — a Father's Day essay contest honoring dads — falls under negative clarification (5) (honoring others). However, the release also touts Bayh's record: 'the Responsible Fatherhood Act, parts of which were signed into law by President Bush earlier this year,' and that as governor he 'more than doubled the amount of money collected for child support payments.' Under clarification (2) and (4), stating that the senator authored legislation that was enacted and touting past achievements with delivered results is credit-claiming, so the release as a whole codes 1 despite the commemorative frame."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords explains why he voted for the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq and criticizes the Administration's lack of an exit strategy. This is an explanation of a vote and criticism of the President — pure position-taking under negative clarification (1). No concrete outcome is claimed as his achievement."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline announces 'SENATE ADOPTS NELSON AMENDMENT,' and the text states his amendment 'passed on a bipartisan vote of 72 to 26' as part of the FY2006 Agriculture Appropriations bill, benefiting Nebraska cattle producers. The boundary rule explicitly states that an amendment adopted is claimed as an accomplishment and counts (clarification 2). This is a clear claim of responsibility for an adopted provision."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy's floor statement introduces 'Medicare for All' legislation ('Today I am introducing legislation to extend Medicare to all Americans') and argues at length against the Administration's health policies. Under negative clarification (1) and (3), introduction of a bill that has not advanced, plus policy argument and criticism, is position-taking, not credit-claiming. No enacted outcome or delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the immediate occasion is a hearing announcement (normally negative clarification 4), the release extensively touts delivered results from the senators' subcommittee oversight: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight, collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415%... to $31 million in 2006,' and 'levies on payments to deadbeat federal contractors have jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' This matches clarification (4) verbatim — touting past achievements with delivered results, including back taxes recovered through oversight — and credit may be shared between the chairman and ranking member."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl 'today co-sponsored legislation' — an amendment to the Budget Resolution that 'would add' 2,000 Border Patrol agents. The conditional 'would' and the absence of any adoption, passage, or inclusion in an advancing package place this under negative clarification (3): cosponsorship/introduction of a measure that has not advanced. No achieved outcome is claimed."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline activity — a letter urging Secretary Chertoff to consider the IBWC levee proposal — is advocacy for future action (negative clarification 2). However, the release also states Cornyn 'added an amendment to the Department of State and Foreign Operations Appropriations bill that required the IBWC... to report to Congress,' i.e., an adopted provision in an appropriations bill. Under the boundary rule, an amendment adopted is a claimed accomplishment, and the positive Durbin example shows that an advocacy letter containing embedded claims of past achieved actions is coded 1. The classification rests on the adopted-amendment claim rather than the letter itself."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The immediate news — introducing a bill to change Alaska's FMAP formula — would alone be negative clarification (3). But the release touts a concrete past achievement: Murkowski 'helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' Under clarifications (2) and (4), claiming a role in enacted legislation that delivered funds to her state is credit-claiming."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces that FEMA is moving 300 travel trailers and 100 mobile homes to Mississippi for Katrina victims, and the release explicitly states: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers.' This fits clarification (3): the agency action is presented as following the senator's appeal/intervention, implying his causal role in delivering a concrete benefit (emergency housing) to his state."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison questioned Army officials at a subcommittee hearing, received assurances that BRAC is on schedule, requested a status report, and 'expressed support' for the Grow the Force request. Questioning at hearings and expressions of support are negative clarifications (2) and (1). Her statement that she is 'encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood' welcomes a decision without claiming a causal role in securing it (negative clarification 6). No achieved outcome is claimed as hers."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the headline action is a letter urging conferees to retain the provision (advocacy), the release centrally claims that 'the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin' to end the Patriot Penalty. Under the boundary rule and clarification (2), an amendment adopted by the chamber is claimed as an accomplishment; Bayh is championing his own Senate-passed provision in an advancing package. Credit may be shared with Durbin."
  }
}

{
  "133": {
    "document_id": "133",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry criticizes the Administration's Dubai Ports deal in light of the parent company's participation in the Israel boycott and demands congressional review. Per negative clarification (1) and (2), criticizing the President and demanding review is position-taking/advocacy; no outcome is claimed as Kerry's achievement."
  },
  "214": {
    "document_id": "214",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "A Pearl Harbor remembrance honoring those who served. Negative clarification (5) treats commemorations as non-credit-claiming; no government action or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "299": {
    "document_id": "299",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Alexander receives a 'Hero of the Taxpayer' award from an advocacy group based on his voting record. This is recognition for past votes, not a claim of responsibility for a concrete government action or delivered outcome. It fits the spirit of negative clarification (5) (awards/honors) and lacks any achieved outcome tied to the senator."
  },
  "490": {
    "document_id": "490",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Webb responds to the NIE on Iran by criticizing the Bush Administration's refusal to engage adversaries. Pure criticism of another actor is position-taking under negative clarification (1); no outcome is claimed."
  },
  "630": {
    "document_id": "630",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd comments on Bush's proposed budget and urges the Administration to fund two additional submarines per year. This is advocacy for future action (negative clarification 2) with no achieved result; the budget is only proposed."
  },
  "721": {
    "document_id": "721",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement on Iraq salutes Iraqi voters and troops, criticizes the President for lacking a plan, and vows Democrats will keep demanding a strategy. This is pure position-taking and criticism under negative clarification (1); no concrete government action or benefit is claimed as Reid's achievement."
  },
  "773": {
    "document_id": "773",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Although the resolution passed the Senate, its substance is honoring the late Senator Eagleton. Negative clarification (5) covers honoring others and commemorations; no concrete benefit to constituents is claimed, so the passage of a honorary resolution is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "1060": {
    "document_id": "1060",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The Senate passed the S-CHIP reauthorization, and the release notes the legislation 'includes an amendment co-sponsored by Senator Nelson' affirming intent to improve small-business health coverage. Under clarification (2), a senator's provision included in an advancing/passing package counts as credit-claiming, distinguishing this from merely applauding passage."
  },
  "1086": {
    "document_id": "1086",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dayton 'applauded the Senate's passage' of added LIHEAP funding while expressing disappointment it falls short; the four amendments he cosponsored to fully fund LIHEAP were all defeated. Under negative clarification (6), welcoming or applauding a decision without claiming a causal role is not credit-claiming, and Dayton's own legislative efforts produced no achieved outcome."
  },
  "1155": {
    "document_id": "1155",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement is largely forward-looking advocacy, but it explicitly touts a past accomplishment bearing his name: 'With the unanimous adoption of the Leahy Hatch medical criteria amendment this committee reached consensus,' which he describes as 'real progress' and one of the pillars 'we painstakingly built.' The codebook's boundary rule states an amendment adopted, claimed as an accomplishment, counts. This is ambiguous because the statement's dominant frame is urging future work (negative clarification 2) and the overall bill never became law, but the explicit claim of an adopted amendment ties Leahy to an achieved outcome."
  },
  "1313": {
    "document_id": "1313",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman expresses frustration at House leaders for not taking up the D.C. voting rights bill and commends Utah officials and Senators Hatch and Bennett for their efforts. This is position-taking, criticism, and praise of others (negative clarification 1); the bill has not advanced and no result is claimed as Lieberman's achievement."
  },
  "1827": {
    "document_id": "1827",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that provisions of Lugar's Good Samaritan Hunger Relief Act 'will be included in the Grassley-Baucus tax relief package' for Katrina victims. Under clarification (2), a senator's specific provisions being included in an advancing package counts as credit-claiming, and the release reinforces this with Lugar's quote appreciating the inclusion of 'this important provision.'"
  },
  "2003": {
    "document_id": "2003",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Clinton 'announced that the Air Force will place a new Predator squadron in New York,' hailing the additional mission and its economic benefits for the state. Gained military missions are an explicitly listed credit-claiming object, and clarification (1) states the announcement frame itself implies responsibility even without the words 'I secured.'"
  },
  "2143": {
    "document_id": "2143",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Murray's statement mourns the passing of former state party chair Karen Marchioro. Condolences fall squarely under negative clarification (5); no policy outcome or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "3047": {
    "document_id": "3047",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release (from Sen. Craig's office) announces USDA's purchase of up to 80 million pounds of potatoes and explicitly notes that 'the Idaho Congressional Delegation wrote the Secretary requesting this action' on March 15, 2005. Under clarification (3), stating that an agency acted after the senator's/delegation's letter is credit-claiming, and credit may be shared with the whole delegation."
  },
  "3201": {
    "document_id": "3201",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces USDA's disaster declaration covering most of Missouri, a listed credit-claiming object (disaster declaration), and the text states he 'worked with USDA officials to expedite the approval of the Governor's request' (clarification 3). It also recalls his earlier announcement of $1,078,500 in ECP funds for Missouri. Clear credit-claiming."
  },
  "3914": {
    "document_id": "3914",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Frist praises national job growth and Republican economic policies while attacking Democrats. This is general position-taking about economic conditions, not a claim of personal responsibility for a concrete government action or outcome benefiting constituents (negative clarification 1)."
  },
  "3950": {
    "document_id": "3950",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The (apparently truncated) text quotes Harkin hoping a new regional diabetes facility will help Iowans, describes the Finley Diabetes Center, and notes Harkin authored the HeLP America Act. No achieved outcome is tied to Harkin: the Act is described only as authored, not enacted, and no funding or designation for the facility is claimed as secured by him. Under the codebook, the senator's name and quote alone are insufficient."
  },
  "4124": {
    "document_id": "4124",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy commends President Bush's call for comprehensive immigration reform and urges action this year. Commending another actor and demanding future action are position-taking/advocacy under negative clarifications (1) and (2); no result has been achieved."
  },
  "4344": {
    "document_id": "4344",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar announces he joined a bipartisan group supporting the Senate-White House PATRIOT Act compromise and says he is 'pleased the compromise includes' library-shielding provisions, but he does not claim to have authored, secured, or caused those provisions. Expressing support for a deal reached by others is position-taking (negative clarifications 1 and 6)."
  }
}
